### 3. Targeted redundant-load-elimination check

This confirms the "reload only when clobbered" rule, including the case where a spill leaves the value in the register and a later block reloads it unnecessarily:

```bash
node - <<'JS'
const { eliminateRedundantReloads } = require('./allocator.js');
const prog = {
  entry: 'A',
  blocks: {
    A: { id:'A', succ:['B'], instrs:[
      {op:'const', dst:'r0', value:7},
      {op:'spill', slot:0, src:'r0', to:'v'},
      {op:'jmp', target:'B'}
    ]},
    B: { id:'B', succ:['C'], instrs:[
      {op:'reload', dst:'r0', slot:0, from:'v'},
      {op:'add', dst:'r1', a:'r0', b:'r0'},
      {op:'jmp', target:'C'}
    ]},
    C: { id:'C', succ:[], instrs:[
      {op:'reload', dst:'r0', slot:0, from:'v'},
      {op:'add', dst:'r2', a:'r0', b:'r0'},
      {op:'ret', src:'r2'}
    ]},
  }
};
const r = eliminateRedundantReloads(prog);
console.log('removed', r.removed);
console.log(JSON.stringify(r.program.blocks.C.instrs));
JS
```

Verified output:

```
removed 2
[{"op":"add","dst":"r2","a":"r0","b":"r0"},{"op":"ret","src":"r2"}]
```

Both reloads are eliminated because the spill in `A` leaves `r0` holding `v`, and no path clobbers `r0` before the reloads. If any instruction wrote `r0` between those points, the facts would be killed and the reload would be retained.

### Allocation report shape

`allocationReport(alloc)` returns one entry per virtual register in the requested format:

```javascript
{
  intervalId: "x0",     // virtual register / interval id
  reg: null,            // physical register, or null when spilled
  spilled: true,
  slot: 2,              // stack slot when spilled
  spills: [             // [{at, kind}]
    { at: "entry#8",  kind: "store"  },
    { at: "body#6",   kind: "reload" },
    { at: "body#8",   kind: "store"  },
    { at: "body#24",  kind: "reload" }
  ]
}
```

Non-spilled intervals get `reg: "r0".."r3"` and an empty `spills` array. The rewritten physical IR is available as `alloc.physical` and is what the differential test interprets.
